ticket_dir (bin/pidly:48) hands its argument straight to find -name, and no
single-ticket command checks it first. `pidly close .n` exits 0 and moves
the number registry into c/, which silently removes the merge-conflict
protection it exists for. A glob such as '*' can match .pidly itself once
the top level is empty.

Done when every command that takes <n> refuses anything but digits, with a
test for `close .n` and `close '*'`.


## Done, 2026-10-24

Ported from a downstream project's vendored copy, where `pidly close '*'`
moved the whole tracker. ticket_dir refuses anything but [0-9]+ and
searches with -mindepth 1; reopen resolves the ticket on its own line so
a failed lookup stops it; new refuses an empty -p ''. tests/args.sh runs
close '*' and close .n, and every command that takes <n> against eight
non-numbers.
